claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Wed, 14 Oct 2026 10:27:30 -0700, Pat Maddox wrote:
>> When using jj maildir-based delivery, I notice that the timestamps are
>> often way off. Look at thread "[PATCH v3 0/6] Add just process-email" as
>> an example:
>
> The Date on a patch is its commit's author date, not when it was
> sent. git format-patch writes each commit's author date into the Date
> header so that git am can restore it, and jj keeps the author date
> across every rebase and rewrite. maildir-deliver.sh then kept any Date
> a message already had and only added one when it was missing.
>
> The v3 thread shows it. All seven files were delivered in the same
> second, 17:11:50 UTC (the maildir file names start with 1791997910).
> Their Date headers say otherwise:
>
> - 0/6, Wed 17:11:01 UTC: when format-patch ran
> - 1/6, Sat 18:09:30 UTC: author date of ktpzmqru
> - 2/6, Sat 18:10:21 UTC
> - 3/6, Sat 19:11:41 UTC
> - 4/6, Sat 19:12:16 UTC
> - 5/6, Wed 10:07:40 UTC (03:07 in your listing)
> - 6/6, Wed 10:08:26 UTC
>
> Each one matches its commit's author timestamp in jj log. ktpzmqru,
> for example, was authored on 2026-10-10 18:09:30 and committed on
> 2026-10-14 17:10:27.
>
>> This makes it hard to find recent messages, or sometimes the patch order
>> is off, and it's all just very confusing.
>
> The order has a second cause. Even with correct dates, a series is
> delivered in one loop and lands within one second, and notmuch orders
> messages with equal dates arbitrarily.
>
>> All messages should have the timestamp of when they are delivered. I'm
>> not sure why this is even a thing. Thoroughly analyze the issue, report
>> on root cause, and provide a deterministic fix so that messages have the
>> time they're actually sent.
>
> The fix is posted as "[PATCH] libexec/mail: Date each message when it
> is delivered" [1]. maildir-deliver.sh now always replaces Date with
> the delivery time, and dates each delivery at least one second after
> the newest file in the maildir, so a series sorts in the order it was
> delivered. It needs no state, because maildir file names already
> start with their delivery time. It also updates the delivery agent
> section of doc/mail-workflow.md and the process-email skill. The
> patch itself was delivered with the new script.
>
> A side effect is that git am takes the author date from Date, so an
> applied patch is dated when it was posted. git send-email behaves the
> same way, since it also rewrites Date to the send time. A burst of N
> messages can be dated up to N seconds ahead of the clock, and a file
> name from a host whose clock runs fast pushes later dates after it.
>
> The prompt.md in "[PATCH 0/6] Answer the inbox with lab-process-mail"
> [2] still says delivery "adds a Date". I will fix that wording in its
> next revision.
